# =====================================================================================================================================
# TEMPLATE (as committed: the PRODUCTION topology, www + apex). The STAGE render of this file has run on the Linux staging host stage.copilt.org since 2026-10-21; the PRODUCTION render is NOT activated anywhere and has never been started on Linux. REHEARSED with a real Caddy 2.11.7 on loopback (docs/cutover/rehearsal.md); `caddy validate` runs on the host in the edge preflight.
# ONE template, three renders (scripts/lib/edge-render.mjs; docs/cutover/stage-linux.md): this committed text is the PRODUCTION topology (www + apex) and is safe by default.
#   production   www.copilt.org + copilt.org (apex redirect), automatic public-CA HTTPS, php_fastcgi -> PHP-FPM on a unix socket. NOT activated (the cutover).
#   stage        ONE public address {$AICC_PUBLIC_HOST} (stage.copilt.org), automatic public-CA HTTPS (Let's Encrypt), no apex block, the stage identity route
#                (/.well-known/aicc-stage-identity, used by the fail-closed test tooling). The Linux staging host renders this mode.
#   rehearsal    the macOS rehearsal: the production topology (or the stage topology) with `local_certs`, loopback ports and either php_fastcgi (scratch php-fpm) or reverse_proxy.
# Nothing but the marked regions (global options, aicc_tls, aicc_site_hook, aicc_stage_identity, wp_proxy, the site addresses, the apex-https block) differs between renders;
# routing, redirects, headers, logging, body caps and error pages are the same text in every mode.
#
# AICC public edge for the www-canonical model (owner decision 2026-10-19):
#   https://www.copilt.org/       the ONLY customer-visible host. One origin per path: static site (Next export) or WordPress (Control Plane).
#   https://copilt.org/*          301 to https://www.copilt.org/* (path and query kept; one hop; 308 for non-GET so a POST keeps its method).
#   http://*                      same, straight to https://www (no http -> https -> www chain).
#   https://api.copilt.org        NOT in this file. It stays its own Caddy site block on the API host (139.59.157.231) and is never proxied here.
#
# TWO LISTENERS (owner decision 2026-10-21, replaces the public CIDR allowlist for /wp-admin/, /wp-login.php and /wp-cron.php):
#   public     :80 / :443 on the host's addresses. /wp-admin/ (except admin-ajax.php and admin-post.php), /wp-login.php (except the GET-only logout shapes) and /wp-cron.php answer 403 here, always.
#   operator   {host}:8443 bound to 127.0.0.1 ONLY (`bind 127.0.0.1`). Nothing on the internet can open a connection to it. The operator reaches it through an SSH tunnel
#              (`ssh -L 443:127.0.0.1:8443`, docs/cutover/stage-linux.md) and a hosts-file entry mapping the site name to 127.0.0.1, so the browser keeps the real host name and gets the
#              same real certificate. A connection that arrives on this listener IS the operator (no header, no X-Forwarded-For and no Host value can make a public request one);
#              a peer that is not loopback is dropped as defence in depth. AICC_OPERATOR_CIDRS (public allowlist) stays an OPTIONAL extra for the macOS rehearsal, EMPTY by default,
#              and the preflight refuses a non-empty value on stage/production.
#
# Why Caddy (recommendation): it issues and renews the certificates itself (automatic HTTPS, HTTP-01/TLS-ALPN-01, no DNS API token), its config is short enough to review line by line,
# and php_fastcgi talks to PHP-FPM over a unix socket (no HTTP upstream port anywhere). nginx would also satisfy docs/unified-experience/domain-migration.md (A11.4); the rules are
# edge-agnostic, see docs/cutover/redirect-matrix.md.
#
# SOURCE OF TRUTH for the redirect/routing blocks: scripts/lib/redirect-matrix.mjs. The blocks between BEGIN GENERATED / END GENERATED are
# written by `node scripts/test-redirects.mjs --write` and checked by `npm run test:redirects`. Do not edit them by hand.
#
# What was reviewed and what was not: `npm run test:redirects` checks the generated blocks and a MODEL of every render (site blocks, imports, matcher order).
# `node scripts/rehearsal/rehearsal.mjs run-tests` replays the same cases through a REAL Caddy (docs/cutover/rehearsal.md). What no rehearsal can prove is
# listed there (public-CA certificate issuance, public DNS, Linux PHP-FPM tuning). `caddy validate` on the real host stays a gate (docs/cutover/runbook.md section 1).
#
# Environment (set in the systemd unit's EnvironmentFile, not in this file; scripts/ops/edge-preflight.sh refuses to start when one is missing or unsafe):
#   AICC_PUBLIC_HOST       stage/production: the public host name (stage: stage.copilt.org; production: www.copilt.org). Required; the preflight refuses a production name in stage mode.
#   AICC_STATIC_ROOT       directory holding the static export, built with  AICC_BASE_PATH="" SITE_URL=https://www.copilt.org npm run build  (the out/ folder; stage: /srv/aicc/site/current)
#                          (the static root must also contain 50x.html: copy docs/cutover/50x.html into every release directory, see the runbook's release procedure)
#   AICC_WP_ROOT           the WordPress docroot the edge reads and PHP-FPM executes (stage: /srv/aicc/cp/current/wp)
#   AICC_PHP_FPM_SOCKET    default unix//run/php/aicc-cp.sock (the socket must exist before Caddy starts; the preflight checks it)
#   AICC_STAGE_ID          stage only: the random id the identity route returns (the test tooling must be given the same value with --stage-id); AICC_STAGE_HOST = AICC_PUBLIC_HOST there
#   AICC_OPERATOR_PORT     optional, default 8443: the loopback operator listener's port
#   AICC_OPERATOR_CIDRS    OPTIONAL extra (macOS rehearsal only), default EMPTY: space-separated CIDRs that count as operators on the PUBLIC listener. REAL-CADDY FINDING: Caddy itself does NOT
#                          fail when this is empty or unset: every operator rule then denies everyone on the public listener, which is exactly what stage/production want.
#   AICC_ACME_EMAIL        [OWNER INPUT] contact address registered with the ACME account. Do not rely on it for expiry warnings: monitor expiry yourself (scripts/ops/cert-expiry-check.sh).
#                          Caddy itself does fail to adapt when it is unset (the `email` line).
#   AICC_ACME_CA           optional, default Let's Encrypt production. Use https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory for the FIRST start (rate limits; browsers will not trust it).
#   AICC_LOG_DIR           optional, default /var/log/caddy (must be writable by the edge's service user; Caddy exits when it is not)
#   AICC_WP_TIMEOUT        optional, default 60s: how long the edge waits for PHP-FPM response headers before answering 504
#   AICC_ADMIN_ADDR        optional, default 127.0.0.1:12019: Caddy's admin API AND its /metrics (loopback or unix socket only; the preflight refuses anything else)
#   AICC_WP_UPSTREAM       rehearsal only (reverse_proxy render): WordPress origin host:port. Not used by php_fastcgi renders.
#
# WordPress prerequisites (otherwise cookies, redirects and absolute URLs break; see docs/cutover/redirect-matrix.md "Hidden dependencies"):
#   * options `home` and `siteurl` = the public origin (stage: https://stage.copilt.org; production: https://www.copilt.org; set BEFORE the first customer: cookie names embed a hash of siteurl)
#   * php_fastcgi gives PHP the REAL client address as REMOTE_ADDR and HTTPS=on from Caddy: wp-config.php needs NO X-Forwarded-Proto line, and AICC_LM_TRUSTED_PROXIES stays EMPTY (default).
#     The fastcgi snippet strips every client-supplied forwarding/identity header (X-Forwarded-*, Forwarded, X-Real-IP, Client-IP, ...) so none can reach PHP as HTTP_*; the reverse_proxy
#     snippet (rehearsal only) still overwrites X-Forwarded-* with edge-made values for an upstream that trusts the edge.
#   * no COOKIE_DOMAIN: cookies stay host-only (never share them with api.copilt.org)
#   * WP-Cron: DISABLE_WP_CRON true plus a system cron `*/2 * * * * wp cron event run --due-now` (every 2 minutes, see infra/systemd/aicc-wp-cron.timer; the entitlement outbox delivery and the daily expiry sweep run on WP-Cron).
#   * TLS: the default is Caddy's automatic HTTPS (the aicc_tls snippet stays empty). Option A (pre-issued certificate files, `tls /path/fullchain.pem /path/privkey.pem` in aicc_tls) still validates
#     because the http:// addresses live in their own blocks, but it is NOT the plan any more (owner decision 2026-10-21: Option B, automatic). Ports 80 and 443 (TCP) must be reachable over
#     IPv4 and IPv6; HTTP/3 is OFF (`servers { protocols h1 h2 }`: no UDP 443, no Alt-Svc). For every rehearsal `tls internal`.
#   * .well-known: only ACME (and the stage identity route in stage renders) is handled by Caddy itself. Search Console HTML verification, security.txt and a future Apple Pay domain-association
#     file must ship in the static site (public/.well-known/, then rebuild) or get an explicit route here. The PRODUCTION render contains NO identity route (npm run test:target-guard checks).
#   * X-Frame-Options SAMEORIGIN is set on everything below. A hosted-iframe payment flow can render OUR order-received page inside the gateway's frame: when a gateway is chosen,
#     drop X-Frame-Options (and use a per-gateway CSP frame-ancestors) for /checkout/* only. Not needed while no gateway is configured.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Global options. The rehearsal generator (scripts/rehearsal/rehearsal.mjs) replaces the region between the BEGIN/END markers with `local_certs`, loopback ports,
# a rehearsal storage directory and `skip_install_trust`; everything else in this file is used unchanged. Production keeps this block.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
{
	# BEGIN GLOBAL OPTIONS
	email {$AICC_ACME_EMAIL}
	# Let's Encrypt only (no ZeroSSL fallback): the owner-approved CA for stage.copilt.org. The first start should use the LE STAGING directory (AICC_ACME_CA) to stay clear of rate limits.
	acme_ca {$AICC_ACME_CA:https://acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory}
	# The admin API can reconfigure the whole edge without authentication: loopback only, and NOT Caddy's default localhost:2019, which another Caddy on the same host (the API's) may own.
	admin {$AICC_ADMIN_ADDR:127.0.0.1:12019}
	# Metrics: Caddy's Prometheus endpoint is served by the ADMIN API only (http://127.0.0.1:12019/metrics, scraped from the host itself). No site routes it (`npm run test:redirects` and the real-Caddy
	# run assert that /metrics on every public route is the normal 404, never metrics text).
	metrics
	# Needed by the `abort` of the operator guard below.
	order abort before redir
	# [OWNER DECIDED 2026-10-21] certificate method: automatic ACME (HTTP-01/TLS-ALPN-01) for the public names; no DNS API token. Needs public DNS pointing here and ports 80/443 open.
	# Never point this file at a public CA from a rehearsal host: the rehearsal generator refuses any acme_ca/email line.
	# REAL-CADDY FINDING: with no explicit setting Caddy answers ANY Host on port 80 with a 308 to https://<that Host>/... (an attacker-chosen host). Every name this edge serves
	# has an explicit http:// address below, so the implicit redirects are switched off and unknown hosts get the catch-all 421 block.
	auto_https disable_redirects
	servers {
		# HTTP/3 OFF (owner decision): HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 only, so no UDP 443 listener and no Alt-Svc header from Caddy.
		protocols h1 h2
		# REAL-CADDY FINDING (round 5): an https request whose Host matches no site (SNI www.copilt.org, `Host: localhost`) is answered by Caddy itself with an EMPTY 200 and `Server: Caddy`.
		# strict_sni_host makes Caddy refuse a request whose Host differs from the TLS server name with 421 (misdirected request) before any route is tried.
		strict_sni_host on
		timeouts {
			read_header 10s
			read_body 60s
			idle 2m
		}
		max_header_size 16KB
	}
	# Runtime + error log. REAL-CADDY FINDING (H-5): Caddy writes the whole request (every header, the full URI) into an error entry (a 502 on a dead PHP-FPM socket carries Nonce / Cart-Token
	# and `?key=...` in clear), so the same redaction as the access log applies: headers deleted, secret-bearing query values redacted. It keeps the 20 MiB x 5 cap: it holds errors, not traffic.
	log {
		output file {$AICC_LOG_DIR:/var/log/caddy}/error.log {
			roll_size 20MiB
			roll_keep 5
			roll_keep_for 720h
		}
		format filter {
			wrap json
			fields {
				request>headers delete
				resp_headers delete
				request>uri regexp "(?i)([?&](?:key|login|user_login|pass|password|pwd|pass1|pass2|token|code|nonce|_wpnonce|reset|license_key|license|secret|auth|signature|sig)=)[^&#]*" "${1}REDACTED"
			}
		}
		level INFO
	}
	# END GLOBAL OPTIONS
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Snippets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-site TLS, imported ONLY by the https site blocks (a `tls <cert> <key>` line in a block that also has http:// addresses is rejected by `caddy validate`:
# "server listening on [...] is HTTP, but attempts to configure TLS connection policies", reproduced by the architecture review). Empty in stage/production = automatic ACME.
# Replaced by `tls internal` in the rehearsal. The operator listener imports it too, so it presents the SAME certificate for the SAME host name (Caddy manages one certificate per name).
# (Option A, pre-issued files, would be:  tls /etc/aicc-edge/certs/fullchain.pem /etc/aicc-edge/certs/privkey.pem. Not the plan.)
(aicc_tls) {
	# stage/production: automatic HTTPS
}

# Hook for rehearsal-only rules (the generator fills it with a "loopback peers only" guard because the rehearsal binds the wildcard address; stage/production leave it empty).
(aicc_site_hook) {
}

# The stage identity route. EMPTY here, and empty in the production render: the production build never contains it. The stage render (and the macOS rehearsal) fill it, so the fail-closed
# test tooling (scripts/lib/target-guard.mjs) can prove that the server it is about to talk to IS the staging host it was told about, before it sends anything that changes state.
(aicc_stage_identity) {
}

# Liveness route for AICC Ops (loopback probe) and the external sentinel: answered by Caddy itself (no upstream, no PHP, no database, no cookies, never cached). It proves the EDGE and its TLS
# certificate answer; it deliberately does NOT prove PHP-FPM or MySQL (probe an app page such as /pricing/ for that). Same in every render so the probe target does not change at cutover.
(aicc_health) {
	@aicc_healthz path /healthz
	handle @aicc_healthz {
		header Cache-Control "no-store"
		respond "ok" 200
	}
}

# Stage-only response header: the staging host must never be indexed (its pages carry the production canonical URL). EMPTY here and in the production render; the stage render fills it
# (scripts/lib/edge-render.mjs). Imported by security_headers, so redirects, branded errors and the 421 catch-all carry it too.
(aicc_stage_robots) {
}

# Operator listener guard: defence in depth on top of `bind 127.0.0.1`. A peer that is not loopback is dropped without an answer.
(aicc_operator_guard) {
	@aicc_op_not_loopback not remote_ip 127.0.0.1 ::1
	abort @aicc_op_not_loopback
}

# Access log. JSON, rotated, and REDACTED by an ALLOW-LIST (architecture review H-5, 2026-10-21: the previous deny-list let the WooCommerce Store API's X-Wp-Nonce / Nonce /
# Cart-Token / Cart-Hash / User-Id headers into the log in clear; a Cart-Token is a JWT that restores a guest cart).
#   * `request>headers` and `resp_headers` are deleted WHOLE. Nothing a client or WordPress puts in a header can reach the log, whatever it is called (a header added by a future plugin,
#     a payment gateway, a different casing). Caddy's filter encoder has no keep-list, so the allow-list is rebuilt from `log_append` placeholders below: exactly these request headers
#     (req_user_agent, req_referer, req_accept_language, req_content_type, req_content_length, req_range, req_if_none_match, req_sec_fetch_site) and these response headers (resp_content_type,
#     resp_content_length, resp_content_encoding, resp_cache_control, resp_location, resp_content_range); the request id is the `request_id` field (the same value as X-Request-Id).
#     Anything not listed here is NOT logged. Adding a field is a reviewed change to this list AND to the real-Caddy test (docs/cutover/rehearsal.md "Logging").
#   * the schema is flat on purpose: fail2ban (infra/fail2ban) and AICC Ops read request.remote_ip / request.method / request.uri / status, which did not move; the appended fields come AFTER them.
#   * no secret-bearing query parameter in any logged URI (WooCommerce password-reset links carry ?key=...&id=..., order links ?key=wc_order_..., the Connect flow ?token=...). The Referer and Location
#     values are filtered the same way because a page opened from a reset link sends it as the Referer of every sub-request.
#   * Cookie, Set-Cookie, Authorization, X-Wp-Nonce, Cart-Token ... are therefore never logged (the old explicit deletes are gone because the whole map is gone).
# Also logged: the real peer address (request.remote_ip), status, duration, a per-request id (the same X-Request-Id sent upstream and back to the client), the upstream that answered
# (empty = served by Caddy itself) and the listener.
# ROTATION AND RETENTION (maths): 50 MiB per file x 10 files kept = a hard cap of about 500 MiB of uncompressed-equivalent log (rolled files are gzip-compressed, so the real footprint is lower),
# plus the live file; the 47 GiB disk cannot fill from it. The cap, not the 30 days, is what limits history. The Linux review measured 20 MiB per ~10 minutes under the load campaign
# (about 2 MiB/min), i.e. 20 MiB x 5 held about ONE HOUR of history under sustained load. The allow-list also shrinks the line: on the rehearsal log the mean line was 1.8 KB with the full header
# blocks and is about 1.0 KB now (an ESTIMATE for the Linux traffic mix, not a measurement of it), so 50 MiB x 10 should hold roughly 6-8 hours at that campaign rate and many days of ordinary
# traffic. It is still a size bound, not a time bound: ship the log off-host if a fixed forensic window is required. After deploying this change, purge the OLD log files on the host
# (they hold the credentials the previous redaction let through; `aicc-edge-*.log.gz` and the live file are rolled out by the reload, then delete the rolled ones).
# Verified by a real run: docs/cutover/rehearsal.md "Logging".
(edge_log) {
	log {
		output file {$AICC_LOG_DIR:/var/log/caddy}/aicc-edge.log {
			roll_size 50MiB
			roll_keep 10
			roll_keep_for 720h
		}
		format filter {
			wrap json
			fields {
				request>headers delete
				resp_headers delete
				req_referer regexp "(?i)([?&](?:key|login|user_login|pass|password|pwd|pass1|pass2|token|code|nonce|_wpnonce|reset|license_key|license|secret|auth|signature|sig)=)[^&#]*" "${1}REDACTED"
				resp_location regexp "(?i)([?&](?:key|login|user_login|pass|password|pwd|pass1|pass2|token|code|nonce|_wpnonce|reset|license_key|license|secret|auth|signature|sig)=)[^&#]*" "${1}REDACTED"
				request>uri regexp "(?i)([?&](?:key|login|user_login|pass|password|pwd|pass1|pass2|token|code|nonce|_wpnonce|reset|license_key|license|secret|auth|signature|sig)=)[^&#]*" "${1}REDACTED"
			}
		}
	}
	log_append request_id {http.request.uuid}
	log_append upstream {http.reverse_proxy.upstream.hostport}
	# which listener answered: `public` or `operator` (import edge_log public | operator)
	log_append listener {args[0]}
	# the request-header allow-list
	log_append req_user_agent {http.request.header.User-Agent}
	log_append req_referer {http.request.header.Referer}
	log_append req_accept_language {http.request.header.Accept-Language}
	log_append req_content_type {http.request.header.Content-Type}
	log_append req_content_length {http.request.header.Content-Length}
	log_append req_range {http.request.header.Range}
	log_append req_if_none_match {http.request.header.If-None-Match}
	# F-09: set by the BROWSER (same-origin | same-site | cross-site | none), not forgeable by a web page; the 4xx-flood jail does not count requests a browser labels cross-site
	log_append req_sec_fetch_site {http.request.header.Sec-Fetch-Site}
	# the response-header allow-list
	log_append resp_content_type {http.response.header.Content-Type}
	log_append resp_content_length {http.response.header.Content-Length}
	log_append resp_content_encoding {http.response.header.Content-Encoding}
	log_append resp_cache_control {http.response.header.Cache-Control}
	log_append resp_location {http.response.header.Location}
	log_append resp_content_range {http.response.header.Content-Range}
}

# Every response, whichever handler produced it (`defer`: applied after the upstream answered, so a header WordPress also sends is REPLACED, never duplicated).
(security_headers) {
	header {
		defer
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin
		X-Frame-Options SAMEORIGIN
		Permissions-Policy "camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()"
		X-Request-Id {http.request.uuid}
		# HTTP/3 is off (servers { protocols h1 h2 }), so Caddy sends no Alt-Svc; the explicit removal keeps it that way even if a future global option turns h3 on by mistake.
		-Alt-Svc
		-Server
		-X-Powered-By
		-Via
	}
	import aicc_stage_robots
	# The edge decides the request id; a client-supplied one is overwritten and the upstream receives this one.
	request_header X-Request-Id {http.request.uuid}
}

# HSTS ramp: start at 300 seconds, raise to 86400, then 31536000 only after the staging pass and a clean week.
# No includeSubDomains and no preload on purpose: api.copilt.org and any staging host manage their own TLS policy, and a preload entry cannot be undone quickly.
(hsts) {
	header {
		defer
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=300"
	}
}

# Static pages only. Report-Only first: the Next export inlines scripts (self.__next_f.push) so 'unsafe-inline' is needed until nonces/hashes exist.
# WordPress paths get NO CSP here: WooCommerce inlines scripts and the payment provider's origins are not known yet (undecided gateway).
(static_csp) {
	header Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; media-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'self'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'"
}

# The one place that talks to WordPress. `wp_proxy` is a SELECTOR: the committed text imports the FASTCGI form (stage, production). The macOS rehearsal generator can switch it to the
# reverse_proxy form (scripts/lib/edge-render.mjs `upstream: "proxy"`) when no php-fpm is available; both forms are modelled and tested, and the Linux target is fastcgi.
# An upstream 5xx (PHP fatal, WordPress critical error page) is NOT shown to the visitor: it becomes the edge's own 5xx page with the same status (handle_errors below).
# Upstream leaks (X-Powered-By, Server) are removed. Do NOT add `trusted_proxies` to the public edge.
(wp_proxy) {
	import wp_proxy_fastcgi
}

# FASTCGI form (Linux): the edge reads the WordPress docroot ({$AICC_WP_ROOT}) and hands PHP files to PHP-FPM over a UNIX SOCKET (no HTTP upstream port exists). Existing files that are not PHP
# (themes, plugin assets, uploads) are served by `file_server` from the same docroot; a path that is not a file falls back to /index.php (WordPress's front controller), like nginx
# `try_files $uri $uri/ /index.php?$args`. Which .php files may run at all is decided by the generated `php_direct` rule (entry points only), BEFORE this snippet is reached.
# PHP sees the REAL client address as REMOTE_ADDR and HTTPS=on (Caddy sets both): nothing needs X-Forwarded-*. Every client-supplied forwarding/identity header is therefore REMOVED from the
# request (not overwritten): measured with a real php-fpm, Forwarded, X-Real-IP, Client-IP and Via from the client reached PHP unchanged without these lines (docs/cutover/rehearsal.md).
(wp_proxy_fastcgi) {
	root * {$AICC_WP_ROOT}
	php_fastcgi {$AICC_PHP_FPM_SOCKET:unix//run/php/aicc-cp.sock} {
		# the docroot is a symlink to the current release: resolve it so opcache is keyed by the real path and a release switch does not serve stale bytecode
		resolve_root_symlink
		dial_timeout 3s
		read_timeout {$AICC_WP_TIMEOUT:60s}
		header_up -X-Forwarded-For
		header_up -X-Forwarded-Proto
		header_up -X-Forwarded-Host
		header_up -Forwarded
		header_up -X-Real-IP
		header_up -X-Client-IP
		header_up -True-Client-IP
		header_up -X-Forwarded-Server
		header_up -X-Original-URL
		header_up -X-Rewrite-URL
		header_up -Client-IP
		header_up -CF-Connecting-IP
		header_up -X-Cluster-Client-IP
		header_up -X-Originating-IP
		header_up -Fastly-Client-IP
		header_up -Forwarded-For
		header_up -X-Forwarded
		header_up -X-Forwarded-Port
		header_up -X-Forwarded-Scheme
		header_up -X-Forwarded-Ssl
		header_up -Front-End-Https
		header_up -X-Url-Scheme
		header_up -Via
		# Security review I1: the list above is a deny-list and deny-lists rot (X-Remote-Addr, X-Remote-IP, X-HTTP-Method-Override and X-Custom-IP-Authorization reached PHP as HTTP_*). Caddy 2.11.7 deletes by
		# prefix (`-Name-*`, proved with a real Caddy and the fastcgi header echo, docs/cutover/rehearsal.md), so whole families of client-controllable identity/rewrite headers are stripped as well.
		header_up -X-Forwarded-*
		header_up -X-Remote-*
		header_up -X-Client-*
		header_up -X-Real-*
		header_up -X-Original-*
		header_up -X-Rewrite-*
		header_up -X-Custom-IP-*
		header_up -X-HTTP-Method-Override
		header_up -X-Method-Override
		header_up -X-HTTP-Method
		header_down -X-Powered-By
		header_down -Server
		# REAL-CADDY FINDING: `error {rp.status_code}` does not take the placeholder (every upstream 5xx became a 500), so one handle_response per status keeps 502/503/504 as they are.
		@up500 status 500
		handle_response @up500 {
			error 500
		}
		@up501 status 501
		handle_response @up501 {
			error 501
		}
		@up502 status 502
		handle_response @up502 {
			error 502
		}
		@up503 status 503
		handle_response @up503 {
			error 503
		}
		@up504 status 504
		handle_response @up504 {
			error 504
		}
	}
	file_server
}

# REVERSE_PROXY form (macOS rehearsal without php-fpm; also the original HTTP-upstream design). The edge OVERWRITES what the client may have sent about itself (X-Forwarded-For / -Proto / -Host are
# set from the connection; Forwarded, X-Real-IP and friends are removed), so WordPress sees only edge-made values: it trusts X-Forwarded-For solely from the edge (AICC_LM_TRUSTED_PROXIES).
(wp_proxy_http) {
	reverse_proxy {$AICC_WP_UPSTREAM} {
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
		header_up X-Forwarded-Host {host}
		header_up -Forwarded
		header_up -X-Real-IP
		header_up -X-Client-IP
		header_up -True-Client-IP
		header_up -X-Forwarded-Server
		header_up -X-Original-URL
		header_up -X-Rewrite-URL
		# More client-identity / scheme headers that code on the origin may read as if they were trusted (WooCommerce's Store API rate limiter reads Client-IP;
		# phase-2 browser rehearsal showed a page-script-supplied value of each of these reached WordPress unchanged). Edge-made values only.
		header_up -Client-IP
		header_up -CF-Connecting-IP
		header_up -X-Cluster-Client-IP
		header_up -X-Originating-IP
		header_up -Fastly-Client-IP
		header_up -Forwarded-For
		header_up -X-Forwarded
		header_up -X-Forwarded-Port
		header_up -X-Forwarded-Scheme
		header_up -X-Forwarded-Ssl
		header_up -Front-End-Https
		header_up -X-Url-Scheme
		header_down -X-Powered-By
		header_down -Server
		transport http {
			dial_timeout 3s
			response_header_timeout {$AICC_WP_TIMEOUT:60s}
			# tls   # uncomment when the upstream is https
		}
		@up500 status 500
		handle_response @up500 {
			error 500
		}
		@up501 status 501
		handle_response @up501 {
			error 501
		}
		@up502 status 502
		handle_response @up502 {
			error 502
		}
		@up503 status 503
		handle_response @up503 {
			error 503
		}
		@up504 status 504
		handle_response @up504 {
			error 504
		}
	}
}

# Request body cap for every PUBLIC WordPress route (login, cart, reset, Store API, license REST, admin-ajax): 12 MB were accepted and forwarded to PHP before. Operators uploading
# media use /wp-admin/ (to_wordpress_admin below); raise the cap here only if a public route legitimately receives bigger bodies. Rejected with 413.
(wp_body_cap) {
	request_body {
		max_size 2MB
	}
}

# WordPress upstream for pages and WordPress's own static files: Cache-Control is whatever WordPress sends (it sends no-store/private for personalised pages and validators for files).
(to_wordpress) {
	import wp_body_cap
	import wp_proxy
}

# Commerce, account and machine paths (cart, checkout, my-account, wp-json, wp-admin, wp-login, wc-ajax, wc-api, wp-cron): the edge forces "never cache" even if WordPress
# forgets (WooCommerce already sends nocache headers). /wp-content/ and /wp-includes/ are deliberately NOT here: they use to_wordpress, so WordPress / the hosting stack decide.
(to_wordpress_nocache) {
	header {
		defer
		Cache-Control "private, no-store, max-age=0"
	}
	import wp_body_cap
	import wp_proxy
}

# /wp-admin/* after the operator allowlist (generated rule wp_admin_big): staff may upload media, so a larger body cap. Public admin-ajax.php / admin-post.php do NOT come here.
(to_wordpress_admin) {
	header {
		defer
		Cache-Control "private, no-store, max-age=0"
	}
	request_body {
		max_size 64MB
	}
	import wp_proxy
}

(to_static) {
	root * {$AICC_STATIC_ROOT}
	file_server
}

